WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.700
Tool calling.

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.967
How a model reaches outside its weights, and why your tool descriptions matter so much.

3
00:00:08.167 --> 00:00:11.533
With tool calling, you expose typed functions to the model.

4
00:00:11.833 --> 00:00:15.800
Each step, it picks one tool and fills in the arguments as JSON.

5
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:19.596
And it chooses by reading names, descriptions, and schemas.

6
00:00:19.596 --> 00:00:20.367
Nothing else.

7
00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:22.867
Let's watch one call.

8
00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:25.567
The user asks about hazardous asteroids.

9
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:30.533
The model reads the tool descriptions, picks neo feed, and fills in the dates.

10
00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:33.300
Your code runs it and returns the result.

11
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.600
Then the model answers from that result.

12
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.833
Four rules make tools reliable.

13
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:42.700
Use tight schemas, so the model can only emit valid shapes.

14
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.367
Write honest descriptions, including when not to use the tool.

15
00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:51.567
Validate inputs, because the model's JSON is a suggestion, not a guarantee.

16
00:00:51.867 --> 00:00:56.467
And execute safely, with timeouts, allow lists, and read only by default.

17
00:00:57.667 --> 00:00:59.733
Here is a tool definition in code.

18
00:01:00.033 --> 00:01:05.433
The description says what the tool does, adds the seven day limit, and says what it is not for.

19
00:01:05.733 --> 00:01:07.400
The schema types both dates.

20
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.633
And both are required, so the model cannot skip them.

21
00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:16.000
Here is a real test with three NASA tools and five varied queries.

22
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:21.567
The first version misroutes two of five, because every description began with the same words.

23
00:01:21.867 --> 00:01:27.133
Rewriting the descriptions to front load the differences takes routing to five out of five.

24
00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:29.064
Let's recap.

25
00:01:29.064 --> 00:01:31.867
The model picks tools by reading descriptions.

26
00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:35.900
So a tool description is prompt engineering, with consequences.

27
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.033
Use tight schemas, and validate every input.

28
00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:42.796
Descriptions are the cheapest accuracy lever you own.

29
00:01:42.796 --> 00:01:45.867
Next up: function calling in the agent context.
